Imagine 1 million users open your app at the same time.
If all requests go to one server, it will crash.
This is where Load Balancer saves the system.
Here is how it works.
𝐒𝐭𝐞𝐩 𝟏
User sends request to Load Balancer
Load Balancer acts as entry point
𝐒𝐭𝐞𝐩 𝟐
Load Balancer distributes requests across multiple servers
Server 1 handles User A
Server 2 handles User B
Server 3 handles User C
𝐒𝐭𝐞𝐩 𝟑
If one server fails, Load Balancer redirects traffic to healthy servers
User never sees failure.
This provides:
𝐇𝐢𝐠𝐡 𝐚𝐯𝐚𝐢𝐥𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲
𝐇𝐢𝐠𝐡 𝐫𝐞𝐥𝐢𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲
𝐇𝐢𝐠𝐡 𝐬𝐜𝐚𝐥𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲
This is used by:
Netflix
Amazon
Google
Uber
Without Load Balancer, modern apps cannot scale.
Most beginners ignore this.
